When a callback’s realm’s global object is a Window whose associated
Document isn’t fully active, Ladybird refuses to invoke it — a callback
function and a callback interface’s operation alike — and throws a
NotSupportedError. Gecko’s CallSetup::GetActiveGlobalObjectForCall()
does the same for a callback whose window fails HasActiveDocument(),
throwing a NotSupportedError (“Refusing to execute function from window
whose document is no longer active.”). Blink’s generated callback
bindings throw “The provided callback is no longer runnable.” when
IsCallbackFunctionRunnable() fails, i.e. once the callback’s context is
invalid or destroyed — and Blink checks the incumbent context too.

WebKit refuses such callbacks without throwing: Its generated callbacks
return UnableToExecute when ActiveDOMCallback::canInvokeCallback()
fails, i.e. once the callback’s ScriptExecutionContext has stopped or
suspended its active DOM objects. But WebKit exempts a few callbacks
with [SkipCallbackInvokeCheck] — NodeFilter’s acceptNode(), e.g. — which
Ladybird, Gecko, and Blink refuse like any other.
